# Lesson 4, new exercise: the same finesse against a queen. AKJ in your hand: lead from dummy and play the jack.
title: Finessing for a queen
id: L04-06
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.1, 3.5
student: S
board: 66
dealer: E
vulnerable: NS

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  K962   643    A85    842
  E  73     Q872   7643   AT6
  S  AQJ84  AKJ    K2     953
  W  T5     T95    QJT9   KQJ7

auction:
  E Pass
  S 1S     = 13+ points, at least four spades
     not *: 18 points and five spades: open your long suit at the one level.
  W Pass
  N 2S     = 6-9 points, 3+ spades
  E Pass
  S 4S     = Game
     not *: You have 19 points counting your doubleton, and partner has at least 6: enough for game.
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1♠ and partner raised to 2♠. With 19 points counting
  distribution, you bid game. You are declarer, because you bid spades
  first, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 4
  losers: 4
  spades: 0 = Nine spades with the ace, king, queen and jack.
  hearts: 1 = The jack, unless East has the queen and you lead toward your ace-king-jack.
  diamonds: 0 = Your king and dummy's ace.
  clubs: 3 = Three small clubs opposite three small clubs.
  remind: You can afford three losers in 4♠. Count the obvious losers suit by suit in your own hand: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: Which loser might go away? Which hand should lead that suit?
play:
  1   W CK  N C2  E C6  S C3
      note: Four losers, one too many. The heart jack might not be one: if East has the queen and you lead hearts from dummy, the jack wins. Nothing to be done about the clubs.
  2   W CQ  N C4  E CT  S C5
  3   W CJ  N C8  E CA  S C9
  4   E D3  S DK  W D9  N D5
      note: Win with your king, and keep dummy's ♦A as a way across.
  5   S SA  W S5  N S2  E S3
      note: Draw trumps. Ace first, then over to dummy's king: you want to end in dummy.
  6   S S4  W ST  N SK  E S7
      note: Trumps are drawn, and you're in dummy.
  7   N H3  E H2  S HJ*  W H5
      note: Lead a heart toward your ace-king-jack.
      HJ key: The finesse. East played low, so play the jack. If East has the queen, the jack wins.
      HJ not HA: That can still work if you come back to dummy and lead hearts toward your jack later. But why not take the finesse now, while you're there? East has played, so if East has the queen, your jack wins.
      HJ not HK: That can still work if you come back to dummy and lead hearts toward your jack later. But why not take the finesse now, while you're there? East has played, so if East has the queen, your jack wins.
  8   S HA  W H9  N H4  E H7
      note: The jack won. Cash the ace and king.
  9   S HK  W HT  N H6  E HQ
      note: And East's queen falls.
  10  S SQ  W C7  N S6  E D4
      note: The rest are yours.
  11  S SJ  W DT  N S9  E D6
  12  S S8  W DJ  N D8  E H8
  13  S D2  W DQ  N DA  E D7
      note: 10 tricks: 4♠ made.

play recap:
  The plan: you counted four losers, one too many. The heart jack was the
  one that might go away, if East held the queen and you led hearts from
  dummy.

  This is the same finesse as against a king, one step down: lead toward
  your ace-king-jack and, when East plays low, play the jack. Because you
  needed to be in dummy to lead hearts, you drew trumps ending there, with
  the ♦A kept in reserve as another way across.
